Coastal wetlands, including marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as natural buffers against the destructive forces of storms. Their ability to reduce flood damage is not merely a matter of standing in the way of water, but rather a complex interaction of physical processes that dissipate energy and manage volume.

The primary mechanism at work is friction. When storm surges and high waves move from the open ocean toward the shore, they encounter the dense vegetation characteristic of wetlands. Plants such as cordgrass, mangrove roots, and reeds create significant drag on the water. This friction slows the flow of the surge,